<p class="gna-lede">An open bite means your upper and lower front teeth do not meet when your back teeth are closed together. In adults it is usually caused by a combination of childhood habits, the way the jaws grew, and sometimes changes in the jaw joints. Depending on the cause and severity, treatment ranges from orthodontics (often with small anchor screws) to orthodontics combined with corrective jaw surgery.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-an-open-bite">What is an open bite?</h2>
<p>Normally, when you bite together, the upper front teeth overlap the lower front teeth by a few millimetres. In an <strong>anterior open bite</strong>, there is a vertical gap between the front teeth even though the back teeth touch. A <strong>posterior open bite</strong>, where some back teeth do not meet, is less common and is often linked to problems with tooth eruption or to one jaw joint.</p>
<p>An open bite can be:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Dental</strong>: the front teeth have not erupted fully or have been pushed apart, but the jaw bones are broadly in proportion.</li>
  <li><strong>Skeletal</strong>: the jaws themselves have grown in a vertical pattern, often with a long lower face, lips that are apart at rest, and a strained chin when the lips are closed.</li>
</ul>
<p>Common day-to-day signs include being unable to bite through food with the front teeth (biting into a sandwich, a dosa edge or a thread of noodles), chewing only with the back teeth, a lisp on "s" and "z" sounds, and heavy wear on the back teeth, which take all the load. Some people are mainly concerned about their smile or the length of their face.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>An open bite can be dental, skeletal, or a mix of both, and the treatment depends on which.</li>
    <li>Childhood thumb-sucking and tongue habits are frequent starting points, but in adults the jaw growth pattern often matters more.</li>
    <li>A bite that starts to open in adulthood needs a jaw-joint assessment before any correction.</li>
    <li>Many adult open bites can be treated with orthodontics and mini-screws; more severe skeletal cases usually need jaw surgery.</li>
    <li>Open bites can relapse, so habits, retainers and follow-up matter as much as the treatment itself.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="causes">What causes an open bite in adults?</h2>
<p>Most adult open bites began in childhood, but the original cause may no longer be obvious. The main contributors are:</p>
<h3>Childhood habits</h3>
<p>Prolonged thumb or finger sucking, or long-term use of a dummy or feeding bottle, can hold the front teeth apart while they are erupting. If the habit stops early, the bite often closes on its own; if it continues for years, the change can persist into adulthood.</p>
<h3>Tongue posture and swallowing pattern</h3>
<p>Some people rest the tongue between the front teeth or push it forward when swallowing (tongue thrust). Whether this causes an open bite or simply adapts to one is still debated, but it can keep a gap open and is a recognised reason for relapse after treatment.</p>
<h3>Mouth breathing and airway</h3>
<p>Long-standing nasal blockage from enlarged adenoids, allergic rhinitis or a deviated septum can encourage mouth breathing and a lower tongue position during growth. The evidence linking this to open bite is mixed, but the airway is usually checked as part of the assessment.</p>
<h3>Jaw growth pattern</h3>
<p>Some people inherit a vertical growth pattern, in which the back of the upper jaw grows down more than usual and the lower jaw rotates open. This produces a skeletal open bite and a long lower face, and it is the main reason why some open bites do not respond fully to braces alone.</p>
<h3>Jaw-joint changes</h3>
<p>The lower jaw hinges at the temporomandibular joints (TMJs). If the rounded upper end of the jaw bone in the joint (the condyle) loses height, from rheumatoid or juvenile arthritis, osteoarthritis or a condition called <strong>condylar resorption</strong>, the back of the jaw shortens and the front teeth separate. This type of open bite can appear or worsen in adulthood, and the joint condition must be assessed and stable before the bite is corrected. Our <a href="/tmj-treatment">TMJ treatment page</a> explains how jaw-joint problems are managed.</p>
<h3>Injury and less common causes</h3>
<p>A fracture of the jaw, particularly near the joints, can suddenly change the bite. Rarely, an open bite is caused by an overgrowing jaw joint or a hormonal condition that enlarges the jaw.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>If your bite suddenly changes after a fall, assault or road accident</strong>, your jaw may be broken. Do not wait for an appointment: go to the nearest emergency department or call 108. Our 24/7 facial-trauma line is +91 90006 66476. Read more about the <a href="/blogs/broken-jaw-symptoms/">symptoms of a broken jaw</a>.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="diagnosis">How an open bite is assessed</h2>
<p>Because the same-looking gap can have very different causes, a careful assessment comes before any treatment plan. This usually includes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>A history of habits, breathing, speech, jaw pain and whether the bite has changed recently</li>
  <li>Examination of the face, lips at rest, smile, tongue position and swallowing</li>
  <li>Examination of the jaw joints and mouth opening</li>
  <li>A lateral cephalogram (side skull X-ray) to measure the vertical proportions of the face and how the jaws relate to each other</li>
  <li>A panoramic X-ray or cone-beam CT (CBCT) scan to look at the teeth, roots and jaw joints</li>
  <li>An MRI of the jaw joints, or repeat imaging over time, when joint disease or condylar resorption is suspected</li>
  <li>An ENT opinion if nasal obstruction or sleep-disordered breathing is likely</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="treatment-options">Treatment options for adults</h2>
<h3>Habit and tongue therapy</h3>
<p>Working with a speech or myofunctional therapist on tongue posture, swallowing and lip seal does not usually close an adult open bite on its own, but it supports other treatment and may reduce relapse.</p>
<h3>Orthodontics: braces or clear aligners</h3>
<p>For dental open bites, orthodontics can bring the front teeth together, and clear aligners can work well in selected mild cases. Simply pulling the front teeth down, however, has limits: it can create a gummy smile and tends to relapse if the underlying skeletal pattern is unfavourable.</p>
<h3>Orthodontics with mini-screws (skeletal anchorage)</h3>
<p><strong>Temporary anchorage devices (TADs)</strong> are small titanium screws, or occasionally mini-plates, placed in the jaw bone under local anaesthesia. They allow the orthodontist to push the back teeth gently up into the bone. As the back teeth move up, the lower jaw rotates closed and the front teeth meet. Systematic reviews suggest this approach can close many adult open bites and reduce lower facial height a little, offering a less invasive option for carefully selected patients. Long-term comparisons with surgery are still limited, so it is not yet clear whether results are equally stable.</p>
<h3>Orthodontics with jaw surgery</h3>
<p>For moderate to severe skeletal open bites, a long face, or open bites combined with an underbite or a receding lower jaw, <a href="/orthognathic-surgery">orthognathic surgery</a> is usually recommended. The most common operation is a <strong>Le Fort I osteotomy</strong>, in which the upper jaw is freed and its back part raised, allowing the lower jaw to swing closed. This may be combined with lower jaw surgery and sometimes a chin procedure. The bones are fixed with small titanium plates and screws under general anaesthesia, through incisions inside the mouth. Orthodontic treatment is done before and after surgery, and the movements are planned in advance, often with <a href="/blogs/role-of-3d-virtual-planning/">3D virtual surgical planning</a>.</p>

