<h2 id="comparison">Treatment options at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Usually suits</th><th>What it changes</th><th>Main limitations</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Habit and tongue therapy</td><td>Everyone with a tongue or lip habit, alongside other treatment</td><td>Tongue posture, swallowing, lip seal</td><td>Rarely closes an adult open bite on its own</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Braces or aligners</td><td>Dental and mild open bites</td><td>Position of the teeth</td><td>Relapse risk; can create a gummy smile if overused</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Braces with mini-screws</td><td>Mild-to-moderate skeletal open bites in selected adults</td><td>Back teeth move up; lower jaw rotates closed</td><td>Screws can loosen or irritate; long-term evidence still developing</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Braces with jaw surgery</td><td>Moderate-to-severe skeletal open bites, long face, combined jaw problems</td><td>Position of the jaw bones and facial proportions</td><td>General anaesthesia, surgical risks, recovery time</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="relapse-and-risks">Relapse, risks and recovery</h2>
<p>Open bites are known for a tendency to reopen, whichever treatment is used. Reviews of surgical correction report that the skeletal change is generally well maintained, but relapse is more likely when tongue habits persist or the original growth pattern was strongly vertical. Long-term retainers, habit control and regular reviews are part of every plan.</p>
<p>Each approach also carries its own risks:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Orthodontics:</strong> gum inflammation if cleaning is difficult, minor root shortening, and relapse without retainers.</li>
  <li><strong>Mini-screws:</strong> local soreness, gum irritation, loosening of a screw that then needs replacing, and occasionally contact with a tooth root.</li>
  <li><strong>Jaw surgery:</strong> swelling and bruising for some weeks, temporary or occasionally lasting numbness of the lips, chin or palate, bleeding, infection, problems with plates, relapse, and changes in jaw-joint symptoms. Our guide to the <a href="/blogs/what-is-orthognathic-surgery-purpose-procedure-and-recovery-explained/">orthognathic surgery procedure and recovery</a> explains what to expect after the operation.</li>
</ul>
<p>After surgery, most people spend one to three nights in hospital, follow a liquid and then soft diet for several weeks, and return to desk work or study in around two to three weeks. Recovery and results vary from person to person.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-a-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Consider an assessment with an orthodontist and a maxillofacial surgeon if:</p>
<ul>
  <li>You cannot bite through food with your front teeth</li>
  <li>Your back teeth are wearing quickly, or chewing feels inefficient</li>
  <li>You have a lisp that has not improved with speech therapy</li>
  <li>Your bite seems to be opening gradually, especially with jaw-joint pain, clicking or stiffness</li>
  <li>An open bite returned after earlier orthodontic treatment</li>
  <li>You are unhappy with a long face or lips that do not close comfortably</li>
</ul>
<p>At Gnathos Facial, Dr. Navatha Mortha and Dr. Suresh P.L. assess the teeth, jaws and jaw joints together, explain which type of open bite you have, and discuss every reasonable option, including those that do not involve surgery. If surgery is right for you, it is carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals in Hyderabad.</p>
